          "text": "# M78: A Lantern Made of Starlight\n\n*In Orion, a quiet patch of dust catches the light of young stars\u2014and gives winter observers a subtle target to seek.*\n\nThe first time you find M78, it may not look like much: a faint smudge in a crowded winter sky. Give it a little time. The glow is not a star shining by itself, but starlight redirected toward us by dust. In a small telescope, that soft, borrowed light can feel less like an object and more like a place: a dim pocket in Orion where darkness has learned to glow.\n\nM78 is also catalogued as NGC 2068. SIMBAD identifies it as a reflection nebula and lists its J2000 position as right ascension 05h 46m 45.8s, declination +00\u00b0 04\u2032 45\u2033. [SIMBAD: M78 / NGC 2068](https://simbad.cds.unistra.fr/simbad/sim-id?Ident=NGC%202068) A reflection nebula shines because nearby stars illuminate its dust; the dust scatters that light into space. Unlike an emission nebula, it is not primarily glowing from its own excited gas. The distinction is easy to miss at the eyepiece, where both kinds of nebula can appear as pale haze. But M78\u2019s light has made a detour: star to dust, dust to telescope, telescope to eye.\n\nTo find it, start with Orion\u2019s Belt. M78 lies northeast of Alnitak, the belt\u2019s easternmost bright star, roughly two and a half degrees away. A finder chart helps, especially in a bright sky. Orion is best placed for evening observing during the Northern Hemisphere\u2019s winter months, when the constellation climbs high enough to view comfortably. Choose a moonless night away from streetlights if you can. The nebula is not an easy naked-eye object; binoculars may show a faint patch under dark skies, while a small telescope at low power can reveal its misty outline. Use averted vision\u2014look slightly to one side of the target rather than directly at it\u2014and give your eyes time to adapt.\n\nDon\u2019t expect a photograph\u2019s saturated blue. Human vision is less sensitive to color in dim light, and a telescope gathers far less light than a long-exposure camera. Instead, look for contrast: a hazy glow against the black sky, perhaps with brighter points nestled within it. Increase magnification only if the image remains steady and bright. Sometimes the most useful observing move is to back away from the eyepiece, let your eyes reset, then return.\n\nThere\u2019s a pleasing paradox in M78. Dust, the material that hides stars in one direction, makes them visible in another. It interrupts the direct path of light and sends some of it outward, where a patient observer can catch it. On a cold night, that faintness can sharpen attention. You begin by searching for a smudge, then notice the stars around it, the shape of the field, the way the darkness seems to gather at its edges.\n\nM78 will not blaze into view. Its invitation is quieter: find Orion, follow the belt, and wait for the scattered light to meet your eye. Sometimes the sky\u2019s lanterns are made not of fire, but of dust.\n\n**Source:** SIMBAD\u2019s entry for NGC 2068 identifies M78 as a reflection nebula and provides its J2000 coordinates: https://simbad.cds.unistra.fr/simbad/sim-id?Ident=NGC%202068"
